WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.200
The greatest common divisor is the largest positive integer dividing both inputs

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:12.967
The Euclidean algorithm repeatedly replaces a pair by the smaller number and the remainder

00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:16.833
Subtracting a multiple preserves exactly the common divisors.

00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:22.289
After dividing eighty-four by thirty, the remainder is twenty-four

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:27.830
Dividing thirty by twenty-four leaves six; dividing twenty-four by six leaves zero

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.833
The last nonzero remainder is six, so the greatest common divisor is six.

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:42.314
Reverse the substitutions to express the divisor as an integer combination of the original numbers

00:00:42.314 --> 00:00:48.058
Six is thirty minus twenty-four, and twenty-four is eighty-four minus twice thirty

00:00:48.058 --> 00:00:52.367
Combining these expressions gives three times thirty minus eighty-four.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:01:00.005
Why does the combination matter? Every common divisor must divide every integer combination, including six

00:01:00.005 --> 00:01:03.807
Since six itself divides both numbers, it is greatest

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:09.300
This is a proof of the answer, not merely an observed remainder pattern.
